window.__courseChapter("v2-04", {"v2-04-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Data Storage** (Vol II). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, Purpose", "md": "A training job can leave expensive accelerators idle even when its models fit in memory and its network has ample capacity, because the next batch must arrive before computation needs it and a saved checkpoint must remain usable when a worker fails. Storage becomes an invisible bottleneck whenever accelerator benchmarks begin with data already in place, excluding the work needed to deliver inputs and preserve results."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "The Fuel Line", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "How ML Workloads Invert Storage Assumpti", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "The ML Storage Hierarchy", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "The Data Pipeline Equation", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "GPUDirect Storage and the CPU Data-Copy ", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Checkpoint Storage", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Versioned Data and Commit Boundaries", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**I/O wall**", "Full-corpus training inverts database assumptions (large sequential shard reads, working sets larger than any cache, synchronized burst writes), so storage is provisioned for streaming bandwidth and burst absorption, and faster compute against a fixed input path plateaus at the delivery rate."], ["**Scoped storage hierarchy**", "The six storage tiers trade bandwidth for capacity and cost but quote bandwidth at different scopes, so a tier ratio is not a feed requirement, and as a cache E[T] = h*T_hot + (1-h)*T_miss means a 95 percent hit rate with a 100x slower miss path delivers about one-sixth of the all-hot rate."], ["**Input delivery budget**", "Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage."], ["**Metadata-bound shard packaging**", "A parallel file system separates a metadata path of a few hundred thousand operations per second from a striped data path, so millions of small files collapse delivery to the metadata rate, and packaging samples into large shards amortizes opens across thousands of samples."], ["**Checkpoint write path**", "A resumable mixed-precision Adam checkpoint is about 14 bytes per parameter, so a coordinated save must write each unique byte once, and tiered staging confines the exposed pause to the local write while the shared-tier copy drains in the background."], ["**Total cost of data delivery**", "Storage cost is capacity plus transfer, request, retrieval, and idle-accelerator cost, and because egress scales with bytes read, tiering is a placement policy chosen from access pattern, read count, and latency need rather than dollars per GB-month."], ["**Versioned artifacts and commit boundaries**", "A fleet data pipeline is a DAG of immutable, content-identified artifacts that lets a run replay the exact batch behind a loss spike, and correctness under worker failure is decided at the output commit boundary by replayable inputs and idempotent writes; generated training data, which the data wall makes necessary, extends the contract with a provenance chain whose retained capacity and verification traffic are budgeted separately, and that lineage is what lets an investigation of model collapse trace the samples involved."], ["**Data stall ratio**", "The data stall ratio measures exposed input wait per step, and it diagnoses the input path only when it is separated from communication and barrier time."], ["**Checkpoint storm**", "When every writer saves at once, synchronized checkpoint bursts saturate the shared storage tier, so save time grows with writer count unless flushes are staggered, rate-limited, or staged locally."], ["**Hedged reads against storage tail latency**", "Sharded reads fan out across many servers so the slowest server sets the step's input latency, and a hedged read to another replica shortens that tail only when its trigger sits in the measured tail, its duplicate load fits spare capacity, and the replicas sit in different failure domains."], ["**Serving-time storage**", "Serving reads bulk weights once per replica, turns a model rollout into a fan-out read burst against shared tiers, and makes retrieval a chain of dependent random reads, so serving storage is sized by load time and per-hop latency rather than epoch throughput."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$2{,}048 \\text{ GPUs} \\times 4{,}096 \\text{ tokens/GPU} \\times 4 \\text{ bytes/token} \\div 0.2\\text{s} \\approx \\mathbf{168 \\text{ MB/s}}$$\n\n$$2{,}048 \\text{ GPUs} \\times 256 \\text{ images/GPU} \\times 116 \\text{ KB/image} \\div 0.2\\text{s} \\approx \\mathbf{303 \\text{ GB/s}}$$\n\n$$\\text{BW}_{\\text{required}} = N_{\\text{GPU}} \\times \\eta_{\\text{target}} \\times \\frac{D_{\\text{vol,batch}}}{T_{\\text{iteration}}}$$\n\n$$\\text{Data Stall \\%} = \\frac{T_{\\text{step}} - T_{\\text{compute}}}{T_{\\text{step}}} \\times 100$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v2-04-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "I/O wall", "back": "Full-corpus training inverts database assumptions (large sequential shard reads, working sets larger than any cache, synchronized burst writes), so storage is provisioned for streaming bandwidth and burst absorption, and faster compute against a fixed input path plateaus at the delivery rate."}, {"front": "Scoped storage hierarchy", "back": "The six storage tiers trade bandwidth for capacity and cost but quote bandwidth at different scopes, so a tier ratio is not a feed requirement, and as a cache E[T] = h*T_hot + (1-h)*T_miss means a 95 percent hit rate with a 100x slower miss path delivers about one-sixth of the all-hot rate."}, {"front": "Input delivery budget", "back": "Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage."}, {"front": "Metadata-bound shard packaging", "back": "A parallel file system separates a metadata path of a few hundred thousand operations per second from a striped data path, so millions of small files collapse delivery to the metadata rate, and packaging samples into large shards amortizes opens across thousands of samples."}, {"front": "Checkpoint write path", "back": "A resumable mixed-precision Adam checkpoint is about 14 bytes per parameter, so a coordinated save must write each unique byte once, and tiered staging confines the exposed pause to the local write while the shared-tier copy drains in the background."}, {"front": "Total cost of data delivery", "back": "Storage cost is capacity plus transfer, request, retrieval, and idle-accelerator cost, and because egress scales with bytes read, tiering is a placement policy chosen from access pattern, read count, and latency need rather than dollars per GB-month."}, {"front": "Versioned artifacts and commit boundaries", "back": "A fleet data pipeline is a DAG of immutable, content-identified artifacts that lets a run replay the exact batch behind a loss spike, and correctness under worker failure is decided at the output commit boundary by replayable inputs and idempotent writes; generated training data, which the data wall makes necessary, extends the contract with a provenance chain whose retained capacity and verification traffic are budgeted separately, and that lineage is what lets an investigation of model collapse trace the samples involved."}, {"front": "Data stall ratio", "back": "The data stall ratio measures exposed input wait per step, and it diagnoses the input path only when it is separated from communication and barrier time."}, {"front": "Checkpoint storm", "back": "When every writer saves at once, synchronized checkpoint bursts saturate the shared storage tier, so save time grows with writer count unless flushes are staggered, rate-limited, or staged locally."}, {"front": "Hedged reads against storage tail latency", "back": "Sharded reads fan out across many servers so the slowest server sets the step's input latency, and a hedged read to another replica shortens that tail only when its trigger sits in the measured tail, its duplicate load fits spare capacity, and the replicas sit in different failure domains."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **I/O wall**?", "options": ["Full-corpus training inverts database assumptions (large sequential shard reads, working sets larger than any cache, synchronized burst writes), so storage is provisioned for streaming bandwidth and burst absorption, and faster compute against a fixed input path plateaus at the delivery rate.", "Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage.", "A resumable mixed-precision Adam checkpoint is about 14 bytes per parameter, so a coordinated save must write each unique byte once, and tiered staging confines the exposed pause to the local write while the shared-tier copy drains in the background.", "The data stall ratio measures exposed input wait per step, and it diagnoses the input path only when it is separated from communication and barrier time."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**I/O wall:** Full-corpus training inverts database assumptions (large sequential shard reads, working sets larger than any cache, synchronized burst writes), so storage is provisioned for streaming bandwidth and burst absorption, and faster compute against a fixed input path plateaus at the delivery rate.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is I/O wall itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Scoped storage hierarchy**?", "options": ["Sharded reads fan out across many servers so the slowest server sets the step's input latency, and a hedged read to another replica shortens that tail only when its trigger sits in the measured tail, its duplicate load fits spare capacity, and the replicas sit in different failure domains.", "The six storage tiers trade bandwidth for capacity and cost but quote bandwidth at different scopes, so a tier ratio is not a feed requirement, and as a cache E[T] = h*T_hot + (1-h)*T_miss means a 95 percent hit rate with a 100x slower miss path delivers about one-sixth of the all-hot rate.", "When every writer saves at once, synchronized checkpoint bursts saturate the shared storage tier, so save time grows with writer count unless flushes are staggered, rate-limited, or staged locally.", "Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Scoped storage hierarchy:** The six storage tiers trade bandwidth for capacity and cost but quote bandwidth at different scopes, so a tier ratio is not a feed requirement, and as a cache E[T] = h*T_hot + (1-h)*T_miss means a 95 percent hit rate with a 100x slower miss path delivers about one-sixth of the all-hot rate.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Scoped storage hierarchy itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Input delivery budget**?", "options": ["Serving reads bulk weights once per replica, turns a model rollout into a fan-out read burst against shared tiers, and makes retrieval a chain of dependent random reads, so serving storage is sized by load time and per-hop latency rather than epoch throughput.", "When every writer saves at once, synchronized checkpoint bursts saturate the shared storage tier, so save time grows with writer count unless flushes are staggered, rate-limited, or staged locally.", "A fleet data pipeline is a DAG of immutable, content-identified artifacts that lets a run replay the exact batch behind a loss spike, and correctness under worker failure is decided at the output commit boundary by replayable inputs and idempotent writes; generated training data, which the data wall makes necessary, extends the contract with a provenance chain whose retained capacity and verification traffic are budgeted separately, and that lineage is what lets an investigation of model collapse trace the samples involved.", "Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Input delivery budget:** Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Input delivery budget itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Metadata-bound shard packaging**?", "options": ["Serving reads bulk weights once per replica, turns a model rollout into a fan-out read burst against shared tiers, and makes retrieval a chain of dependent random reads, so serving storage is sized by load time and per-hop latency rather than epoch throughput.", "A parallel file system separates a metadata path of a few hundred thousand operations per second from a striped data path, so millions of small files collapse delivery to the metadata rate, and packaging samples into large shards amortizes opens across thousands of samples.", "Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage.", "Sharded reads fan out across many servers so the slowest server sets the step's input latency, and a hedged read to another replica shortens that tail only when its trigger sits in the measured tail, its duplicate load fits spare capacity, and the replicas sit in different failure domains."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Metadata-bound shard packaging:** A parallel file system separates a metadata path of a few hundred thousand operations per second from a striped data path, so millions of small files collapse delivery to the metadata rate, and packaging samples into large shards amortizes opens across thousands of samples.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Metadata-bound shard packaging itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Checkpoint write path**?", "options": ["When every writer saves at once, synchronized checkpoint bursts saturate the shared storage tier, so save time grows with writer count unless flushes are staggered, rate-limited, or staged locally.", "A resumable mixed-precision Adam checkpoint is about 14 bytes per parameter, so a coordinated save must write each unique byte once, and tiered staging confines the exposed pause to the local write while the shared-tier copy drains in the background.", "A fleet data pipeline is a DAG of immutable, content-identified artifacts that lets a run replay the exact batch behind a loss spike, and correctness under worker failure is decided at the output commit boundary by replayable inputs and idempotent writes; generated training data, which the data wall makes necessary, extends the contract with a provenance chain whose retained capacity and verification traffic are budgeted separately, and that lineage is what lets an investigation of model collapse trace the samples involved.", "Sharded reads fan out across many servers so the slowest server sets the step's input latency, and a hedged read to another replica shortens that tail only when its trigger sits in the measured tail, its duplicate load fits spare capacity, and the replicas sit in different failure domains."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Checkpoint write path:** A resumable mixed-precision Adam checkpoint is about 14 bytes per parameter, so a coordinated save must write each unique byte once, and tiered staging confines the exposed pause to the local write while the shared-tier copy drains in the background.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Checkpoint write path itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["I/O wall: Full-corpus training inverts database assumptions (large sequential shard reads, working sets larger than any cache, synchronized burst writes), so storage is provisioned for streaming bandwidth and burst absorption, and faster compute against a fixed input path plateaus at the delivery rate.", "Scoped storage hierarchy: The six storage tiers trade bandwidth for capacity and cost but quote bandwidth at different scopes, so a tier ratio is not a feed requirement, and as a cache E[T] = h*T_hot + (1-h)*T_miss means a 95 percent hit rate with a 100x slower miss path delivers about one-sixth of the all-hot rate.", "Input delivery budget: Required input bandwidth is the number of distinct consumers times target compute fraction times batch bytes over compute time, every stage of the chain must sustain it because delivered rate is the minimum over stages, and prefetch absorbs variance but never a sustained deficit; a direct DMA path from storage into accelerator memory (GPUDirect Storage, for example) removes the host-copy stage only when that copy is the limiting stage.", "Metadata-bound shard packaging: A parallel file system separates a metadata path of a few hundred thousand operations per second from a striped data path, so millions of small files collapse delivery to the metadata rate, and packaging samples into large shards amortizes opens across thousands of samples."]}], "terms": [], "id": "v2-04-00-overview", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v2-04-01-the-fuel-line": {"title": "The Fuel Line", "summary": "Distinguish storage input demand from internal HBM traffic when interpreting the bandwidth gap.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["The Fuel Line"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The Fuel Line** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Distinguish storage input demand from internal HBM traffic when interpreting the bandwidth gap\n- Analyze why ML storage architecture requires tiered placement that simultaneously satisfies bandwidth physics and capacity economics\n- Evaluate why compute and network benchmarks in isolation fail to guarantee scalable end-to-end training throughput without storage pipeline validation\n- Explain the systems consequences of compute throughput scaling significantly faster than persistent storage bandwidth\n- Explain the technical term for synchronized cluster-wide write bursts generated during distributed training state saves"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § The Fuel Line", "md": "The dense accelerator nodes of a later section pack eight GPUs delivering petaFLOP/s of aggregate compute, and the InfiniBand fabrics of a later section connect thousands of such nodes at hundreds of gigabits per second. Within the multi-tier fleet stack of a later section, data storage completes that physical foundation, staging training data, model weights, optimizer state, and intermediate checkpoints at the right distance from the accelerator. An engine without fuel is expensive sculpture."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: HBM-to-NVMe bandwidth gap as the central constraint; checkpoint volume at frontier scale (2.45 TB resumable state, 10.6 PB lifetime traffic); physics-economics tradeoff forcing a tiered hierarchy; compute scaling outpacing storage bandwidth growth."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A 175B-parameter model trains for 30 days on 256 nodes, writing resumable mixed-precision Adam checkpoints of roughly \\$2.45\\text{ TB}$ (\\$350\\text{ GB}$ FP16 weights, \\$700\\text{ GB}$ FP32 master weights, and \\$1.4\\text{ TB}$ of Adam moments) every 10 minutes. Explain why ML storage design is fundamentally a joint physics-and-economics optimization problem.", "md": "HBM supplies high local bandwidth for active tensors but cannot economically retain the corpus and checkpoint history. Shared durable storage pools capacity at lower cost, while interfaces, contention, and client allocation determine the rate and latency a consumer receives. Tier placement must satisfy input-delivery and checkpoint-recovery budgets at the lowest total delivery cost. Remote input reads can suffice when their sustained rate and buffered latency meet demand; local staging is useful when they do not. Compare capacity cost, transfers, and exposed accelerator wait rather than infer a stall from remote placement alone."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An H100 accelerator consumes data from its HBM at roughly \\$3.35\\text{ TB/s}$, while a single local NVMe drive delivers sequential reads at roughly \\$7\\text{ GB/s}$. A team is designing the storage infrastructure for a 1,000-accelerator training fleet. Which conclusion follows directly from this bandwidth ratio?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. HBM and NVMe serve different traffic: model state and intermediate tensors can be reused many times for each input batch. The roughly \\$479× ratio illustrates tier disparity, not a storage-rate requirement. Batch bytes, compute time, concurrency, and buffering determine whether inputs arrive in time; neither RAID aggregation nor a faster network alone establishes that condition.", "type": "single", "options": ["The gap is easily bridged by standard NVMe RAID arrays because sequential read throughput scales without bound across drives.", "The roughly \\$479× device-bandwidth gap motivates tiered staging, but required input bandwidth must be calculated from batch bytes and compute time rather than equated with HBM traffic.", "The bandwidth gap is negligible because large-scale training is entirely compute-bound and rarely exercises HBM bandwidth.", "The bandwidth gap disappears when high-speed InfiniBand fabrics deliver data directly into HBM from remote storage endpoints without local staging."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish storage input demand from internal HBM traffic when interpreting the bandwidth gap"]}, {"q": "A cluster benchmark demonstrating that compute hardware achieves advertised peak TFLOP/s and network fabric achieves advertised bisection bandwidth is sufficient evidence that end-to-end training throughput will scale linearly with accelerator count.", "explanation": "False. Isolated compute and fabric benchmarks evaluate floating-point throughput and inter-node communication in synthetic conditions without exercising the end-to-end data pipeline. Real training throughput requires continuously streaming input batches, loading model weights, and absorbing periodic checkpoint write storms; if the storage hierarchy cannot deliver input samples within the compute window (IronLawComputeTime), accelerators sit idle regardless of peak compute capability.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why compute and network benchmarks in isolation fail to guarantee scalable end-to-end training throughput without storage pipeline validation"]}, {"q": "An accelerator upgrade reduces compute time per batch while the input representation and storage delivery time remain fixed. What is the primary systems consequence for training cluster design?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Faster computation leaves less time to fetch the next batch. Overlap and prefetching hide latency within their buffering window, but cannot fix a sustained service-rate deficit. Automatic compression is not guaranteed, removing staging does not improve a slow source, and checkpoint frequency does not set input delivery throughput.", "type": "single", "options": ["Training pipelines automatically increase data compression ratios to prevent accelerator starvation without requiring architectural adjustments.", "Storage hierarchies can eliminate intermediate DRAM and NVMe caching tiers because modern network fabrics directly absorb the difference.", "Checkpoint intervals must be decreased proportionally to the compute growth to keep local NVMe buffers from overflowing.", "The compute window available to deliver each batch shrinks, so input I/O can become the bottleneck unless the measured delivery path keeps pace."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the systems consequences of compute throughput scaling significantly faster than persistent storage bandwidth"]}, {"q": "In large-scale distributed training, when hundreds of nodes simultaneously pause computation to write gigabytes of model weights and optimizer states to persistent storage, the resulting synchronized burst of write traffic is called a ____.", "explanation": "The correct term is checkpoint storm. A checkpoint storm occurs when all data-parallel or model-parallel workers simultaneously flush their memory shards to local or remote storage, creating extreme write contention that must be absorbed via tiered staging (such as writing to local NVMe before asynchronously copying to a parallel file system).", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct term is checkpoint storm"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the technical term for synchronized cluster-wide write bursts generated during distributed training state saves"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now distinguish storage input demand from internal HBM traffic when interpreting the bandwidth gap.", "You can now analyze why ML storage architecture requires tiered placement that simultaneously satisfies bandwidth physics and capacity economics.", "You can now evaluate why compute and network benchmarks in isolation fail to guarantee scalable end-to-end training throughput without storage pipeline validation.", "You can now explain the systems consequences of compute throughput scaling significantly faster than persistent storage bandwidth."]}], "id": "v2-04-01-the-fuel-line", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-02-how-ml-workloads-invert-storage": {"title": "How ML Workloads Invert Storage Assumptions", "summary": "Evaluate storage procurement decisions against the sequential-streaming and bursty-write inversions of ML workloads.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["How ML Workloads Invert Storage Assumptions"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **How ML Workloads Invert Storage Assumptions** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Evaluate storage procurement decisions against the sequential-streaming and bursty-write inversions of ML workloads\n- Analyze why temporal-locality caching strategies such as LRU fail on uniformly streamed ML pretraining datasets\n- Justify shard-level shuffling under explicit mixing and convergence checks\n- Classify model rollout storage traffic as a synchronized fan-out read burst and quantify its cluster-wide volume\n- Explain why phase-dependent read/write behavior requires a multi-tier storage hierarchy"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § How ML Workloads Invert Storage Assumptions", "md": "A database administrator moving to an ML infrastructure team would find many familiar storage design principles inverted by the multi-terabyte sequential scans and checkpoint bursts of the 175B running example. Transactional databases emphasize small random accesses and strong consistency, issuing thousands of 4 KB reads per second to serve user queries."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: the five workload inversions (streaming, massive working sets, bursty writes, phase-dependent ratios, uniform access); IOPS trap and sequential vs random access disparities; LRU hit-rate failure on uniformly shuffled datasets; shard-level shuffling as a convergence-throughput compromise."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why ML data loaders implement shard-level shuffling combined with within-shard buffer mixing rather than true global per-sample random shuffling across petabyte-scale training corpora.", "md": "Shard-level shuffling and within-shard buffer mixing preserve sequential I/O while introducing sample-order variation. Adequate mixing depends on shard composition and buffer size: homogeneous shards may require finer shards or larger mixing buffers. Validate the layout against the loss curve of a fully shuffled baseline on a subset before committing the run."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A storage procurement team is sizing storage hardware for a cluster dedicated to pretraining foundation models on a \\$200 TB immutable text corpus. Which purchasing decision represents the most severe misallocation of budget based on ML workload characteristics?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Paying a premium for enterprise SSDs optimized for high \\$4 KB random-read IOPS while under-provisioning sustained sequential read bandwidth (GB/s). ML pretraining streams large contiguous batches sequentially, where a drive delivers \\$7-14 GB/s, whereas \\$4 KB random reads drop throughput to approx 0.5 GB/s; optimizing for random IOPS buys capabilities the workload never utilizes while starving the pipeline of raw gigabytes-per-second throughput. Shard aggregation is the standard mitigation against metadata serialization, local NVMe caching prevents repeated network egress, and sizing for checkpoint bursts directly addresses the bursty write inversion.", "type": "single", "options": ["Paying a premium for enterprise SSDs optimized for high \\$4 KB random-read IOPS while under-provisioning sustained sequential read bandwidth (GB/s).", "Aggregating raw text samples into \\$256\\text{ MB}$ to \\$4\\text{ GB}$ contiguous binary shards prior to training to amortize file-system metadata operations.", "Provisioning local NVMe drives on compute nodes to serve as a warm read cache for multi-epoch training passes.", "Sizing the parallel file system write tier to absorb synchronized multi-terabyte checkpoint write bursts from hundreds of nodes."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate storage procurement decisions against the sequential-streaming and bursty-write inversions of ML workloads"]}, {"q": "An in-memory LRU cache sized to \\$300 GB placed in front of a \\$3 TB uniformly shuffled pretraining dataset will achieve roughly a \\$90\\% hit rate after the first epoch warms the cache because all samples are reused across epochs.", "explanation": "False. A cache holding one-tenth of a uniformly shuffled corpus does not gain a 90 percent hit rate merely because samples recur in later epochs. Most cached samples are evicted before reuse. A fixed cyclic scan larger than the cache can approach zero hits; independently reshuffled epochs allow low but nonzero cross-epoch hits that depend on cache fraction and sample order.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why temporal-locality caching strategies such as LRU fail on uniformly streamed ML pretraining datasets"]}, {"q": "When an inference platform deploys a new version of a 175B-parameter model (\\$350 GB FP16 weights) across 100 serving replicas, how does the storage access profile contrast with a training checkpoint save?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. It creates a synchronized fan-out read burst of roughly \\$35\\text{ TB}$ (\\$100 \\times 350\\text{ GB}$) that must complete within the deployment latency budget while serving traffic continues. A model rollout is the architectural mirror image of a checkpoint storm: instead of many nodes simultaneously writing state to storage (fan-in burst), many replica nodes simultaneously read the same large model weights from storage (fan-out burst). The small-random-writes option describes OLTP database journaling. The delta-streaming option assumes parameter-level differential rollouts that production serving frameworks avoid in favor of immutable weight images. The central-write option confuses read distribution to serving nodes with training checkpoint writes.", "type": "single", "options": ["It generates continuous small random writes to update parameter journals on each replica node.", "It streams an incremental delta of only modified parameters over hours to minimize bandwidth utilization.", "It creates a synchronized fan-out read burst of roughly \\$35\\text{ TB}$ (\\$100 \\times 350\\text{ GB}$) that must complete within the deployment latency budget while serving traffic continues.", "It requires sustained multi-gigabyte-per-second sequential write bandwidth to central durable object storage."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: classify model rollout storage traffic as a synchronized fan-out read burst and quantify its cluster-wide volume"]}, {"q": "A team plans one storage configuration for the whole life of a language-model project: tokenizing and deduplicating a \\$3\\text{ TB}$ compressed corpus into \\$6\\text{ TB}$ of shards, then training on those shards with periodic checkpoints. Why does the chapter conclude that no single configuration optimizes every phase?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The fourth inversion is that the read/write ratio shifts by lifecycle phase: preprocessing scans and rewrites large artifacts, while training streams read-only shards between synchronized checkpoint bursts. Each phase loads the tiers differently, which is why ML systems use a multi-tier hierarchy rather than one storage technology. Training reads are not small random lookups, the tokenized corpus is larger than the compressed source, and checkpoints are bursty large writes, not continuous small ones.", "type": "single", "options": ["Preprocessing reads and writes large sequential artifacts in MapReduce-like passes, while training reads immutable shards between bursty checkpoint writes, so the read/write balance and access pattern change by phase.", "Training reads are dominated by small random lookups that only a database-style random-IOPS tier can serve, while preprocessing is purely sequential.", "The tokenized corpus is smaller than the compressed source, so preprocessing frees capacity that the training phase cannot use.", "Checkpoint writes are continuous and small, so they require a different write-ahead log configuration than preprocessing."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain why phase-dependent read/write behavior requires a multi-tier storage hierarchy"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now evaluate storage procurement decisions against the sequential-streaming and bursty-write inversions of ML workloads.", "You can now analyze why temporal-locality caching strategies such as LRU fail on uniformly streamed ML pretraining datasets.", "You can now justify shard-level shuffling under explicit mixing and convergence checks.", "You can now classify model rollout storage traffic as a synchronized fan-out read burst and quantify its cluster-wide volume."]}], "id": "v2-04-02-how-ml-workloads-invert-storage", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-03-the-ml-storage-hierarchy": {"title": "The ML Storage Hierarchy", "summary": "Order the six storage tiers by placement and distinguish that order from a bandwidth comparison.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["The ML Storage Hierarchy", "Bandwidth cliffs between tiers", "Tier 0 GPU HBM", "Tier 1 host DRAM", "Tier 2 local NVMe", "Data formats for training", "Tier 3 parallel file systems", "Tier 4 object storage", "Tier 5 archive and cold storage"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The ML Storage Hierarchy** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Order the six storage tiers by placement and distinguish that order from a bandwidth comparison\n- Explain the architectural function of pinned host memory in accelerating CPU-to-GPU DMA data transfers\n- Analyze parallel file system metadata saturation from telemetry signatures and prescribe shard-aggregation remediation\n- Justify RAID-0 storage configurations for warm cache tiers and identify failure boundaries where lack of redundancy causes unrecoverable data loss\n- Distinguish retained corpus capacity from combined sampled-batch and transformation demand when sizing multimodal delivery"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § The ML Storage Hierarchy", "md": "No single storage device can hold a petabyte-scale training corpus, absorb multi-terabyte checkpoints, and still feed an accelerator at terabytes per second. HBM supplies high local bandwidth at limited capacity, whereas object storage pools durable capacity behind a much slower service and client path. A multi-tier hierarchy combines these resources rather than requiring one technology to meet every demand. Each tier offers a different balance of bandwidth, latency, capacity, and cost. The hierarchy extends the familiar processor memory hierarchy across nodes."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Bandwidth cliffs between tiers\n- Tier 0 GPU HBM\n- Tier 1 host DRAM\n- Tier 2 local NVMe\n- Data formats for training\n- Tier 3 parallel file systems\n- Tier 4 object storage\n- Tier 5 archive and cold storage\n\nPay attention to: six-tier physical hierarchy (HBM, Host DRAM, Local NVMe, PFS, Object, Archive); bandwidth cliffs and staging buffer dynamics; Host DRAM's multi-stage pipeline and pinned memory role; NVMe RAID-0 warm cache durability semantics."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A training node uses four local NVMe drives configured in RAID-0 as a warm cache for \\$25 TB of pre-shuffled training shards. Explain why RAID-0 is an appropriate engineering trade-off for this specific tier, and identify what workload change would make RAID-0 catastrophic.", "md": "RAID-0 aggregates drive bandwidth without parity capacity overhead, making it appropriate for a disposable warm cache when a durable source remains available and the fleet can tolerate restaging after a drive failure. It does not protect availability: losing one drive can invalidate the array. If the array holds the only copy of an unrecoverable artifact or a checkpoint not yet durably replicated, a drive failure can permanently lose that state; the cache placement policy cannot substitute for a recovery contract."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Order the six tiers from Tier 0 through Tier 5 in the chapter's placement hierarchy, moving outward from accelerator-resident memory through node-local resources to shared storage and archive:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (4) GPU HBM, (3) Host DRAM, (1) Local NVMe SSD, (5) Parallel File System, (2) Object Storage, (6) Archive / Cold Storage. HBM is accelerator-resident; host DRAM and local NVMe serve the node; the parallel file system provides shared access; object storage retains durable organizational data; and archive preserves rarely accessed history. This is the chapter's placement order, not a universal bandwidth ranking. HBM rates are per accelerator, DRAM and local NVMe rates are per node, and shared-service rates must be allocated among clients and checked against the actual delivery path.", "type": "order", "items": ["GPU HBM", "Host DRAM", "Local NVMe SSD", "Parallel File System", "Object Storage", "Archive / Cold Storage."], "hints": ["Think about: order the six storage tiers by placement and distinguish that order from a bandwidth comparison"]}, {"q": "Host DRAM (Tier 1) executes a four-stage data loader pipeline: Read, Decode, Augment, and Collate. Why place the final collated batch in page-locked ('pinned') host memory for direct asynchronous DMA to GPU HBM without an intermediate staging copy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Pinned memory locks physical pages in host RAM so the PCIe DMA controller can copy data directly to GPU memory without an intermediate pinned staging copy. Standard virtual memory pages can be paged out or relocated by the operating system, forcing the GPU driver to copy data into an intermediate pinned staging buffer before initiating DMA; allocating pinned memory directly (`pin_memory=True` in PyTorch) eliminates this extra CPU memory copy and allows direct DMA across PCIe. The compression option is incorrect as pinned memory is an OS page-locking mechanism, not a compression algorithm. The NUMA replication claim is false because pinning prevents paging rather than replicating data or choosing NUMA placement. The ACID durability option confuses volatile host DRAM staging with persistent transaction logging.", "type": "single", "options": ["Pinned memory automatically compresses tensors using host CPU vector units before PCIe transmission.", "Pinned memory locks physical pages in host RAM so the PCIe DMA controller can copy data directly to GPU memory without an intermediate pinned staging copy.", "Pinned memory replicates the batch across all NUMA nodes to ensure uniform memory access for parallel data loader threads.", "Pinned memory guarantees transactional ACID durability on host storage in case the worker process crashes during transfer."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural function of pinned host memory in accelerating CPU-to-GPU DMA data transfers"]}, {"q": "A vision training cluster migrating to a high-performance parallel file system (PFS) with \\$500\\text{ GB/s}$ rated aggregate bandwidth reports that throughput during ImageNet training is capped at under \\$5\\text{ GB/s}$, with storage data disks showing negligible I/O activity. The dataset consists of millions of individual JPEG files. A worker trace shows repeated waits for file opens, and the MDS reports a sustained operation queue with rising lookup latency while its service rate is saturated. What bottleneck do these additional measurements establish, and which fix addresses it?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The saturated MDS service rate, rising lookup latency, and worker file-open waits establish a metadata bottleneck in this case. Repackaging images into sequential shards amortizes per-file operations across many samples. Idle data disks and low throughput alone would not establish this cause; the supplied metadata measurements distinguish it from a slow decoder or another blocked pipeline stage.", "type": "single", "options": ["The network fabric is dropping packets due to congestion; enable flow control on top-of-rack switches.", "The parallel file system is bottlenecked on drive endurance wear-leveling; replace SSDs with high-DWPD enterprise drives.", "The dataset is stored as millions of individual image files, saturating the Metadata Server (MDS) with per-file POSIX lookups; repackage images into large sequential shards (such as WebDataset tar archives).", "The GPUs are compute-bound and cannot consume data faster; decrease the batch size to increase I/O request frequency."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze parallel file system metadata saturation from telemetry signatures and prescribe shard-aggregation remediation"]}, {"q": "A multi-modal training job combines \\$3\\text{ TB}$ of text, \\$50\\text{ TB}$ of images, and \\$200\\text{ TB}$ of video. The infrastructure team provisions storage bandwidth based exclusively on the video modality because video represents over \\$75\\%$ of total dataset capacity. What can be concluded about this sizing decision from the supplied information?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Retained corpus capacity does not determine per-step delivery demand. Sampling frequency, batch representation, and compute time set how many bytes each modality contributes to the feed, while transformations determine its decoding and augmentation work. Sum those demands across active streams and compare them with measured stage rates and buffer capacity. Video-only sizing omits that accounting, but the supplied corpus sizes do not prove a particular storage, host-DRAM, or decoding bottleneck.", "type": "single", "options": ["Adequacy cannot be established from corpus capacity alone; sizing must include the combined sampled-batch byte rate and decoding, augmentation, and buffering demand of all active modalities.", "The video stream will starve the parallel file system because image and text files use incompatible POSIX stripe sizes.", "The model will fail to converge because object storage cannot maintain ACID consistency when serving multiple modalities simultaneously.", "The training job will exhaust GPU HBM because video tensors automatically overwrite text embeddings during collation."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish retained corpus capacity from combined sampled-batch and transformation demand when sizing multimodal delivery"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now order the six storage tiers by placement and distinguish that order from a bandwidth comparison.", "You can now explain the architectural function of pinned host memory in accelerating CPU-to-GPU DMA data transfers.", "You can now analyze parallel file system metadata saturation from telemetry signatures and prescribe shard-aggregation remediation.", "You can now justify RAID-0 storage configurations for warm cache tiers and identify failure boundaries where lack of redundancy causes unrecoverable data loss."]}], "id": "v2-04-03-the-ml-storage-hierarchy", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-04-the-data-pipeline-equation": {"title": "The Data Pipeline Equation", "summary": "Apply the pipeline throughput equation to predict aggregate storage bandwidth requirements as cluster compute scales.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["The Data Pipeline Equation", "Pipelining and prefetching", "Multi-worker data loading", "Data locality and placement"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The Data Pipeline Equation** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the pipeline throughput equation to predict aggregate storage bandwidth requirements as cluster compute scales\n- Calculate the exposed data stall time when I/O fetch duration exceeds accelerator computation time under pipelining\n- Calculate minimum prefetch buffer depth from P99 I/O latency and iteration compute duration\n- Evaluate why average bandwidth metrics fail to rule out storage bottlenecks without fine-grained tail-latency and stall-ratio profiling\n- Analyze how locality-aware scheduling optimizes node recovery times and mitigates shared storage contention at fleet scale"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § The Data Pipeline Equation", "md": "Whether a training job streams from object storage or reads from local NVMe, each worker's next batch must reach HBM before the current compute step finishes; falling short starves the fleet's accelerators, while provisioning far above demand strands capital in idle storage bandwidth. Sizing the hierarchy therefore begins by turning the accelerator consumption rate into a required byte rate. The data pipeline throughput equation in a later section quantifies the bandwidth the pipeline must sustain for a target training rate:"}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Pipelining and prefetching\n- Multi-worker data loading\n- Data locality and placement\n\nPay attention to: the pipeline throughput equation (linear scaling with GPU count); data stall ratio diagnostic metric and latency hiding limits; prefetch buffer sizing to P99 tail latency ($Q_{\\text{prefetch}} = \\lceil T_{\\text{I/O,p99}} / T_{\\text{compute}} \\rceil$); multi-worker CPU allocation heuristics ($W \\approx 4 \\times G$)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A training iteration has a GPU compute time of $T_{\\text{compute}} = 200\\text{ ms}$ and an average I/O fetch time of $T_{\\text{I/O}} = 250\\text{ ms}$ per batch. Explain why enabling double-buffering (pipelining batch $i+1$ during batch $i$ compute) reduces but cannot eliminate the data stall, and quantify the remaining stall time per step.", "md": "Pipelining overlaps I/O with compute such that step time becomes $T_{\\text{step}} = \\max(T_{\\text{compute}}, T_{\\text{I/O}}) = T_{\\text{compute}} + \\max(0, T_{\\text{I/O}} - T_{\\text{compute}})$. Because $T_{\\text{I/O}} (250\\text{ ms}) > T_{\\text{compute}} (200\\text{ ms})$, the pipeline remains fundamentally I/O-bound: when the GPU completes its 200 ms forward-backward pass, the next batch is still 50 ms away from completing its transfer. Pipelining reduces step time from sequential execution (\\$200 + 250 = 450\\text{ ms}$) to \\$250\\text{ ms}$, but an exposed stall of \\$50\\text{ ms}$ (a \\$20\\%$ stall ratio, calculated as $(250 - 200) / 250 \\times 100$) persists until storage bandwidth is increased or I/O volume is reduced."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "The data pipeline throughput equation is RequiredInputBandwidth = InputConsumerAcceleratorCount × TargetComputeTimeFraction × fracBatchDataVolumeIronLawComputeTime. If a cluster doubles its number of GPUs consuming distinct local batches from 1,024 to 2,048 while keeping per-GPU batch size, uninterrupted compute time per batch, and target active-time fraction constant, how does the required aggregate storage bandwidth scale?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. It doubles (\\$2×) because aggregate byte consumption scales strictly linearly with the number of accelerators independently executing forward-backward iterations. In the pipeline throughput equation, each data-parallel accelerator requires an independent batch BatchDataVolume every compute window IronLawComputeTime, so doubling InputConsumerAcceleratorCount exactly doubles the total bytes per second that the storage system must deliver to the fleet. The constant-bandwidth option confuses dataset size with per-iteration ingestion rate. The quadratic scaling option confuses inter-GPU all-reduce communication with storage data loader ingestion. The halving option misinterprets worker count scaling.", "type": "single", "options": ["It remains constant because data-parallel workers divide the fixed total dataset into smaller partitions.", "It quadruples (\\$4×) because collective communication overhead scales quadratically with worker count.", "It decreases by \\$50\\% because each node requires fewer samples per epoch.", "It doubles (\\$2×) because aggregate byte consumption scales strictly linearly with the number of accelerators independently executing forward-backward iterations."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: apply the pipeline throughput equation to predict aggregate storage bandwidth requirements as cluster compute scales"]}, {"q": "A data loader pipeline has an accelerator compute window of $T_{\\text{compute}} = 200\\text{ ms}$ per batch. The storage tier exhibits median I/O latency of \\$180\\text{ ms}$ but experiences P99 tail-latency spikes of \\$500\\text{ ms}$ due to network jitter. What is the minimum recommended prefetch buffer depth ($Q_{\\text{prefetch}}$) suggested by the P99 sizing rule, before allowing for longer or correlated stalls?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Three ready batches cover \\$600\\text{ ms}$ of computation, exceeding the stated \\$500\\text{ ms}$ P99 delay. This is a buffer-sizing baseline, not a guarantee: a longer or correlated outage can still drain the queue, and insufficient sustained delivery bandwidth cannot be fixed by buffering. The median and worker count alone do not bound the required coverage.", "type": "single", "options": ["At least 3 batches ($Q_{\\text{prefetch}} = \\lceil 500 / 200 \\rceil = 3$) plus a safety margin, covering the stated P99 delay when the queue is full.", "Exactly 1 batch, because median I/O latency (\\$180\\text{ ms}$) is strictly less than compute time (\\$200\\text{ ms}$).", "Zero batches, because multi-worker asynchronous threading eliminates the need for host-memory queue buffering.", "At least 10 batches, because queue depth must equal the total number of CPU worker threads allocated to the data loader."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate minimum prefetch buffer depth from P99 I/O latency and iteration compute duration"]}, {"q": "If a cluster monitoring dashboard indicates that average storage read bandwidth matches the aggregate throughput predicted by the pipeline equation, storage performance issues can be conclusively ruled out as the cause of low GPU utilization.", "explanation": "False. Average bandwidth metrics mask transient tail-latency spikes, worker-side lock contention, metadata serialization, and prefetch buffer underruns. Even if aggregate gigabytes-per-second over a 10-minute window matches the theoretical target, microsecond-level and millisecond-level I/O jitter can cause individual GPUs to stall repeatedly at step boundaries, driving down utilization. Diagnostics must evaluate per-step wait-for-data distributions (P95/P99 stall times) rather than aggregate bandwidth averages alone.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why average bandwidth metrics fail to rule out storage bottlenecks without fine-grained tail-latency and stall-ratio profiling"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain how locality-aware scheduling reduces cluster startup latency and relieves bandwidth pressure on the shared parallel file system when a node fails during a 1,024-GPU training run.", "md": "A locality-aware scheduler can place a replacement worker where the required immutable input shards already reside in local NVMe, avoiding their transfer from the shared file system and reducing recovery traffic. This accelerates the data-staging part of recovery; it does not eliminate checkpoint restoration, coordination, or process startup. Locality is useful only when the cached version is correct and the placement also satisfies the job's resource constraints."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Compare the three primary data staging patterns (prestaging, on-demand staging, and pure streaming) in terms of job startup latency and network bandwidth consumption during multi-epoch training.", "md": "Prestaging copies the required working set before training, trading startup delay and cache capacity for fewer remote input reads during execution. On-demand staging fetches shards on first access and can reuse them in later epochs if the cache retains them. Pure streaming avoids a full-dataset staging phase but repeatedly uses the remote path and remains exposed to its tail latency. Neither on-demand staging nor streaming eliminates first-batch latency, and local staging does not eliminate unrelated network traffic such as checkpoint writes."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the pipeline throughput equation to predict aggregate storage bandwidth requirements as cluster compute scales.", "You can now calculate the exposed data stall time when I/O fetch duration exceeds accelerator computation time under pipelining.", "You can now calculate minimum prefetch buffer depth from P99 I/O latency and iteration compute duration.", "You can now evaluate why average bandwidth metrics fail to rule out storage bottlenecks without fine-grained tail-latency and stall-ratio profiling."]}], "id": "v2-04-04-the-data-pipeline-equation", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-05-gpudirect-storage-and-the-cpu-da": {"title": "GPUDirect Storage and the CPU Data-Copy Bypass", "summary": "Explain the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage compared to traditional CPU-mediated I/O.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["GPUDirect Storage and the CPU Data-Copy Bypass", "The complete data path"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **GPUDirect Storage and the CPU Data-Copy Bypass** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage compared to traditional CPU-mediated I/O\n- Analyze the technical preconditions and workload characteristics required for GPUDirect Storage to deliver throughput gains\n- Evaluate the architectural boundaries of GPUDirect Storage and distinguish local DMA acceleration from remote network latency\n- Calculate the CPU core savings delivered by GPUDirect Storage and evaluate its impact on host preprocessing capacity\n- Analyze the multi-hop data movement and CPU mediation steps in the traditional storage I/O path"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § GPUDirect Storage and the CPU Data-Copy Bypass", "md": "Even when the storage tier has enough raw bandwidth, the traditional data path for loading training data routes every shard read from storage $\\to$ host DRAM $\\to$ GPU HBM in three hops, one of them a CPU copy inside host DRAM. Data is first read from NVMe into a kernel buffer in host DRAM by a DMA transfer, then copied by the CPU into the data loader's user-space buffer, and finally transferred over PCIe to GPU memory by the GPU's DMA engine."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- The complete data path\n\nPay attention to: DMA-based bypass mechanism vs traditional 3-hop bounce-buffer path; latency reduction (\\$120\\ mus to 30\\ mus) and CPU core savings; workload suitability (high transfer rate, GPU-decodable data) vs CPU-bound decode pipelines; GDS scope boundaries (accelerates local/RDMA NVMe, does not eliminate cloud object store latency)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A computer vision training pipeline uses complex CPU-based JPEG decompression and multi-step data augmentations (color jitter, affine warping). Explain why adopting GPUDirect Storage (GDS) without moving decompression to the GPU yields minimal end-to-end throughput improvement.", "md": "GDS optimizes the payload data transfer path by enabling direct DMA from storage to GPU memory, eliminating host DRAM bounce copies. However, if the data is stored in compressed formats (like JPEG) that must be decoded and transformed by host CPU cores, the raw bytes must still pass through CPU registers and host memory for decompression before tensor assembly; bypassing host DRAM with GDS is only beneficial if the data is already in GPU-consumable format (uncompressed/tokenized tensors) or if decoding and augmentation are offloaded to GPU hardware decoders (such as NVIDIA DALI)."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Which statement precisely describes the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage (GDS)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. GDS establishes a direct DMA transfer path between NVMe storage (local or NVMe-oF) and GPU memory, eliminating the CPU-mediated bounce buffer in host DRAM while retaining CPU control-plane coordination. In the traditional path, data is copied from NVMe to a kernel buffer, copied by the CPU to user space, and then transferred to the GPU via PCIe DMA; GDS programs a direct peer-to-peer DMA between the NVMe controller and GPU HBM, skipping host DRAM payload copies while the CPU continues to issue and manage I/O control requests. The HBM caching option is physically impossible given dataset-to-HBM capacity ratios. The flash compression claim mischaracterizes GDS as in-storage compute. The bounce-buffer routing option describes the exact traditional bottleneck GDS was designed to eliminate.", "type": "single", "options": ["GDS caches the entire training corpus in GPU HBM to eliminate all storage I/O operations during training epochs.", "GDS compresses training tensors directly inside the NVMe flash controller using hardware acceleration.", "GDS routes network packets through host DRAM bounce buffers to perform real-time data integrity checksumming.", "GDS establishes a direct DMA transfer path between NVMe storage (local or NVMe-oF) and GPU memory, eliminating the CPU-mediated bounce buffer in host DRAM while retaining CPU control-plane coordination."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage compared to traditional CPU-mediated I/O"]}, {"q": "Enabling GPUDirect Storage (GDS) eliminates the physical round-trip network latency and P99 tail-latency spikes associated with fetching shards directly from remote cloud object storage endpoints.", "explanation": "False. GDS optimizes the local or RDMA-attached PCIe/NVMe-over-Fabrics data transfer path by eliminating CPU-mediated bounce-buffer copies. It has no effect on the underlying physical network latency, cross-datacenter transit times, or multi-tenant congestion inherent in remote cloud object storage services like Amazon S3 or Google Cloud Storage, which still require tiered prefetch buffering or local caching.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the architectural boundaries of GPUDirect Storage and distinguish local DMA acceleration from remote network latency"]}, {"q": "In a high-throughput vision training node with 8 GPUs processing a combined 64,000 images per second, traditional CPU-mediated I/O requires \\$120\\ mus of CPU time per image (consuming approx 7.68 CPU seconds per wall-clock second, or approx 8 dedicated CPU cores). GDS reduces CPU overhead to \\$30\\ mus per image (\\$1.92 CPU seconds per second, or approx 2 cores). What is the primary systems benefit of this 'CPU bypass dividend'?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. It frees approximately 6 CPU cores per node that can be reallocated to computationally intensive data augmentation and pipeline orchestration without adding hardware. By offloading raw memory-copy and interrupt-handling work to hardware DMA engines, GDS reduces per-transfer CPU overhead by \\$75\\% (from \\$120\\ mus to \\$30\\ mus), freeing CPU cores to perform complex data augmentations that improve model convergence without throttling the input pipeline. The tape archive option is absurd for active training feeds. The GPU power reduction claim is false because GDS changes storage transfer overhead, not GPU arithmetic energy. The PCIe bus bandwidth claim confuses hardware physical link capacity with software transfer efficiency.", "type": "single", "options": ["It enables the cluster to eliminate all local NVMe SSDs in favor of remote tape archives.", "It frees approximately 6 CPU cores per node that can be reallocated to computationally intensive data augmentation and pipeline orchestration without adding hardware.", "It reduces GPU power consumption by \\$75\\% during tensor matrix multiplication.", "It doubles the physical PCIe bus bandwidth between the CPU socket and the GPU accelerators."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the CPU core savings delivered by GPUDirect Storage and evaluate its impact on host preprocessing capacity"]}, {"q": "Order the sequence of operations that occurs in the traditional (non-GDS) storage I/O path when an accelerator requests a batch from local NVMe:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (1) The data loader process issues a POSIX read() system call traversing the VFS and filesystem drivers, (2) The NVMe controller initiates a DMA transfer of raw blocks into an OS kernel buffer in host DRAM, (3) The host CPU copies payload bytes from kernel space to user-space pinned memory, (4) The CPU programs a PCIe DMA transfer from host DRAM to GPU HBM, (5) The accelerator launches compute kernels on the newly arrived tensor resident in HBM. The traditional path begins with a file-system read call (1), stages data into an OS kernel buffer via storage DMA (2), requires a CPU-mediated memory copy into user space (3), executes a second DMA transfer over PCIe to GPU memory (4), and finally enables GPU computation (5).", "type": "order", "items": ["The NVMe controller initiates a DMA transfer of raw blocks into an OS kernel buffer in host DRAM", "The host CPU copies payload bytes from kernel space to user-space pinned memory", "The CPU programs a PCIe DMA transfer from host DRAM to GPU HBM", "The accelerator launches compute kernels on the newly arrived tensor resident in HBM."], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the multi-hop data movement and CPU mediation steps in the traditional storage I/O path"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage compared to traditional CPU-mediated I/O.", "You can now analyze the technical preconditions and workload characteristics required for GPUDirect Storage to deliver throughput gains.", "You can now evaluate the architectural boundaries of GPUDirect Storage and distinguish local DMA acceleration from remote network latency.", "You can now calculate the CPU core savings delivered by GPUDirect Storage and evaluate its impact on host preprocessing capacity."]}], "id": "v2-04-05-gpudirect-storage-and-the-cpu-da", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-06-checkpoint-storage": {"title": "Checkpoint Storage", "summary": "Explain how tiered checkpoint staging minimizes the exposed training pause time by decoupling local NVMe writes from asynchronous durable replication.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["Checkpoint Storage", "Synchronous and asynchronous checkpoint writes", "Checkpoint retention and restore"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Checkpoint Storage** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain how tiered checkpoint staging minimizes the exposed training pause time by decoupling local NVMe writes from asynchronous durable replication\n- Evaluate the difference between write-completion acknowledgments and end-to-end restore validation in checkpoint reliability\n- Analyze how ZeRO-3 state sharding reduces cluster checkpoint write volume and eliminates multi-hour checkpoint storm stalls\n- Design a tiered checkpoint retention policy that balances rapid crash recovery against long-term storage capacity costs\n- Analyze the multi-stage execution workflow of tiered asynchronous checkpointing"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § Checkpoint Storage", "md": "After the … accelerators of the running cluster have been training for six hours, a power supply failure turns checkpoint storage into the difference between resuming within minutes and repeating hours or days of lost computation. Whether the job recovers cleanly depends on three storage questions: how quickly the most recent checkpoint was saved, where its shards reside, and whether they can be read back intact."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Synchronous and asynchronous checkpoint writes\n- Checkpoint retention and restore\n\nPay attention to: checkpoint volume calculations (175B resumable Adam state = 2.45 TB); tiered checkpoint staging mechanics (local NVMe write vs async PFS copy); ZeRO-3 sharded checkpointing vs naive full-replica checkpoint storms; checkpoint retention policies and restore verification imperatives."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A cluster of 1,024 accelerators trains a 70B-parameter model whose resumable checkpoint contains \\$140 GB of FP16 weights, \\$280 GB of FP32 master weights, and \\$560 GB of Adam moments, or \\$980 GB per full replica. Contrast naive full-replica checkpointing with ZeRO-3 sharded checkpointing across a \\$100 GB/s shared storage fabric.", "md": "Naive full-replica checkpointing makes all 1,024 replicas write the same \\$980 GB state, generating \\$1,003.52 TB of traffic and taking roughly \\$10,035 s, or \\$167.3 minutes, across a \\$100 GB/s fabric. ZeRO-3 writes one logical copy partitioned across the workers, collapsing total write volume to \\$980 GB and shared-fabric time to roughly \\$9.8 s. Gradients are absent from both totals because the restored job recomputes them."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A 256-node cluster saves a 175B-parameter resumable checkpoint (\\$2.45 TB total: \\$350 GB FP16 weights, \\$700 GB FP32 master weights, and \\$1.4 TB of Adam moments) every 10 minutes. Under tiered staging, each node writes its \\$9.57 GB ZeRO-3 shard to local NVMe (\\$28 GB/s local array) in approx 0.34 s, followed by an asynchronous background copy to a \\$1 TB/s parallel file system taking approx 2.45 s. What is the exposed training pause time (T_write) per checkpoint?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Roughly \\$0.34 s, because the training barrier releases immediately after the fast local NVMe write completes, while PFS replication proceeds asynchronously. The synchronous phase is \\$9.57 GB / 28 GB/s approx 0.342 s, about \\$0.057\\% of a 10-minute interval. The 2.8-second and 2.45-second options incorrectly block training on the shared-tier transfer. Zero seconds is impossible because the job must still capture and persist a consistent local snapshot. This is a local-write estimate excluding snapshot and coordination overhead. The checkpoint remains vulnerable to node loss until it reaches its durability boundary; retain the previous committed generation during background replication.", "type": "single", "options": ["Roughly \\$2.8 s, because training must remain paused until both the local write and the durable PFS copy complete.", "Roughly \\$0.34 s, because the training barrier releases immediately after the fast local NVMe write completes, while the PFS replication proceeds asynchronously in the background.", "Zero seconds, because modern storage drivers execute zero-copy writes without interrupting GPU tensor execution.", "Roughly \\$2.45 s, because parallel file system latency determines the minimum barrier synchronization time across nodes."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain how tiered checkpoint staging minimizes the exposed training pause time by decoupling local NVMe writes from asynchronous durable replication"]}, {"q": "In distributed ML infrastructure, storage reliability is defined primarily as a write-completion property: once all nodes successfully execute and acknowledge their checkpoint write calls, the checkpoint can be guaranteed reliable for cluster recovery.", "explanation": "False. Storage reliability is a restore property, not a write property. A checkpoint is only reliable if the infrastructure continuously and automatically validates that saved shards can be located, reconstructed, schema-validated, and restored under realistic preemption and recovery conditions; unvalidated backups frequently fail during real incidents due to silent corruption, missing metadata, or software version mismatches.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the difference between write-completion acknowledgments and end-to-end restore validation in checkpoint reliability"]}, {"q": "A 30-day training run of a 175B model generates 4,320 resumable checkpoints totaling roughly \\$10.6 PB of state data. Which retention policy balances rapid failure recovery, long-term auditability, and storage cost without exhausting the parallel file system budget?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Keeping the 3 most recent checkpoints on the parallel file system bounds active high-performance storage to \\$7.35 TB (\\$3 × 2.45 TB), while archiving every 100th checkpoint preserves 43 historical snapshots in object storage. Retaining all \\$10.6 PB on the PFS wastes high-cost capacity; deleting all checkpoints eliminates restart protection; and tape-only retention imposes excessive recovery latency.", "type": "single", "options": ["Retain all 4,320 checkpoints on the parallel file system to provide granular step-by-step rollback capabilities throughout the entire 30-day run.", "Delete all intermediate checkpoints immediately after each step and rely exclusively on live memory replication across peer nodes for fault tolerance.", "Retain the 3 most recent checkpoints on the parallel file system for fast local recovery, copy every 100th checkpoint to cloud object storage for long-term lineage, and purge older intermediate snapshots.", "Compress all checkpoints with gzip and write them directly to cold tape archive without maintaining any copies on NVMe or parallel file systems."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: design a tiered checkpoint retention policy that balances rapid crash recovery against long-term storage capacity costs"]}, {"q": "Order the sequence of steps executed during a tiered asynchronous checkpointing operation:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) The training orchestrator initiates a synchronized step barrier across all worker ranks, (3) Each rank snapshots its model weights and optimizer state shard into a local pinned DRAM buffer, (4) The training barrier releases and accelerators immediately resume forward-backward computation for the next step, (5) Each node flushes its pinned memory snapshot to local NVMe at full drive bandwidth, (1) Background daemons asynchronously transfer the local checkpoint shards from NVMe to the shared parallel file system. The process begins with a coordinated barrier (2), creates a fast in-memory snapshot (3), releases the compute barrier to resume training (4), writes the snapshot to local NVMe in the background (5), and asynchronously replicates the shard to shared storage in the background (1).", "type": "order", "items": ["The training orchestrator initiates a synchronized step barrier across all worker ranks", "Each rank snapshots its model weights and optimizer state shard into a local pinned DRAM buffer", "The training barrier releases and accelerators immediately resume forward-backward computation for the next step", "Each node flushes its pinned memory snapshot to local NVMe at full drive bandwidth.", "Background daemons asynchronously transfer the local checkpoint shards from NVMe to the shared parallel file system"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the multi-stage execution workflow of tiered asynchronous checkpointing"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain how tiered checkpoint staging minimizes the exposed training pause time by decoupling local NVMe writes from asynchronous durable replication.", "You can now evaluate the difference between write-completion acknowledgments and end-to-end restore validation in checkpoint reliability.", "You can now analyze how ZeRO-3 state sharding reduces cluster checkpoint write volume and eliminates multi-hour checkpoint storm stalls.", "You can now design a tiered checkpoint retention policy that balances rapid crash recovery against long-term storage capacity costs."]}], "id": "v2-04-06-checkpoint-storage", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-07-versioned-data-and-commit-bounda": {"title": "Versioned Data and Commit Boundaries", "summary": "Apply stable, idempotent writes at the output commit boundary to make replay after a worker failure effectively exactly-once.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["Versioned Data and Commit Boundaries", "Replay and commit boundaries in data pipelines", "The synthetic fuel line"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Versioned Data and Commit Boundaries** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply stable, idempotent writes at the output commit boundary to make replay after a worker failure effectively exactly-once\n- Distinguish persistent storage amplification from read and write traffic in a verified synthetic-data pipeline\n- Justify retaining synthetic-sample provenance to support audits and filtering when recursive training degrades model quality\n- Explain the mechanism of model collapse caused by recursive training on uncurated synthetic data"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § Versioned Data and Commit Boundaries", "md": "If a multi-week training run suffers a sudden loss spike or a deployed model regresses, an engineering team that did not record which shard versions, preprocessing code, and shuffle seeds fed each step cannot tell a model bug from a silent change in the input data. A checkpoint manifest provides one commit boundary per save. The data pipeline that built the training corpus has many, one at every stage that turns raw inputs into processed shards."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Replay and commit boundaries in data pipelines\n- The synthetic fuel line\n\nPay attention to: output commit boundaries and effectively exactly-once replay through stable, idempotent writes; the Data Wall and the shift from human to synthesized training data; provenance chain requirements (generator, filters, judge models, scores); synthetic-data capacity (\\$1.4\\times$) versus pipeline traffic (\\$4.4\\times$)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why synthetic training data pipelines must treat storage as an active 'provenance engine' by recording the generating model, filters, and verifier scores for each accepted sample, rather than storing plain text files.", "md": "If synthetic data is stored as unverified plain text without lineage, future models risk recursively training on uncurated, erroneous synthetic outputs produced by prior generations, triggering 'model collapse' where model diversity and output quality progressively degrade. Storing an immutable provenance chain (generating model ID, prompt template, rejection filters, and verifier reward scores) supports auditing so data can be filtered, re-weighted, or purged when a generator or judge model is found to possess systematic errors or biases."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A feature-pipeline worker writes the output row for click event `e42`, then crashes before it records that the write succeeded. Its replacement cannot tell whether the row was committed. Which design lets the replacement reprocess `e42` without losing the event or counting it twice?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The duplicate comes from the worker's own replay, not from message delivery, so it must be removed at the output commit boundary: a replayed write under a stable key leaves the same result however many times it repeats. At-most-once processing loses `e42` instead of duplicating it, a reliable transport cannot see the worker's replay, and a watermark governs event-time completeness rather than replay correctness.", "type": "single", "options": ["Write each output row under a stable key such as `(feature_version, e42)`, so a replay overwrites the same row instead of appending a second one.", "Switch the stage to at-most-once processing, so uncertain events are never replayed.", "Deliver messages between workers over a reliable transport, so every message arrives exactly once.", "Lengthen the watermark delay, so late events have more time to arrive before the window closes."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: apply stable, idempotent writes at the output commit boundary to make replay after a worker failure effectively exactly-once"]}, {"q": "A team generates a \\$1\\text{ TB}$ synthetic text dataset. Retained provenance adds \\$40\\%$ metadata overhead, and 3 verifier models each read the payload once without materializing another full copy. What persistent capacity and total pipeline traffic are required?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Persistent capacity is \\$1.4\\text{ TB}$: the \\$1\\text{ TB}$ payload plus \\$0.4\\text{ TB}$ of provenance. Pipeline traffic is \\$4.4\\text{ TB}$: those \\$1.4\\text{ TB}$ of writes plus three \\$1\\text{ TB}$ verifier reads. Verification passes do not multiply capacity unless their outputs are materialized as additional full datasets.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$1.4 TB of persistent capacity and \\$4.4 TB of traffic: \\$1 TB payload write, \\$0.4 TB metadata write, and \\$3 TB of verifier reads.", "Exactly \\$1.0 TB, because verifier models run entirely in GPU registers without generating persistent storage artifacts.", "Roughly \\$0.4 TB, because synthetic data compresses more efficiently than natural language text.", "Roughly \\$14\\text{ TB}$, because every verifier model stores a full \\$350\\text{ GB}$ copy of its internal neural activations for every sample."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish persistent storage amplification from read and write traffic in a verified synthetic-data pipeline"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply stable, idempotent writes at the output commit boundary to make replay after a worker failure effectively exactly-once.", "You can now distinguish persistent storage amplification from read and write traffic in a verified synthetic-data pipeline.", "You can now justify retaining synthetic-sample provenance to support audits and filtering when recursive training degrades model quality.", "You can now explain the mechanism of model collapse caused by recursive training on uncurated synthetic data."]}], "id": "v2-04-07-versioned-data-and-commit-bounda", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-08-storage-economics": {"title": "Storage Economics", "summary": "Calculate the break-even point and cost savings of local NVMe data staging versus repeated cloud object storage streaming.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["Storage Economics", "Tiering strategies"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Storage Economics** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the break-even point and cost savings of local NVMe data staging versus repeated cloud object storage streaming\n- Justify storage infrastructure capital investments by quantifying the opportunity cost of storage-induced accelerator idle time\n- Evaluate build-versus-buy trade-offs for large-scale storage infrastructure using capital expenditure, operational costs, and data egress fees\n- Explain the concept of data egress fees charged when reading datasets across cloud network boundaries"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § Storage Economics", "md": "Checkpoints, training shards, and the lineage that binds them now each have a place in the hierarchy, and every placement carries a price set by the tier that holds the bytes and by how often they cross a billing boundary. ML storage economics is therefore not simply about choosing the cheapest capacity tier; the design objective is to minimize total cost of data delivery, which combines recurring storage charges, cross-boundary transfer fees, request and retrieval fees, and the opportunity cost of idle accelerators."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Tiering strategies\n\nPay attention to: Total Cost of Data Delivery vs raw capacity storage price; egress fees, request costs, and idle GPU opportunity costs (the storage iceberg); stream vs stage economics and break-even epoch calculations; build vs buy tradeoffs for on-prem parallel file systems vs cloud object storage."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In a 1,000-GPU cluster where compute time is billed at $\\$2.00$ per GPU-hour, an undersized storage system introduces an additional 20 percentage points of input stalls (reducing the active compute fraction from \\$90\\%$ to \\$70\\%$). Calculate the daily cost of this lost compute capacity and explain why investing in high-performance storage is economically rational.", "md": "At the stated rate, the cluster costs $\\$48,000$ per day. An additional 20 percentage points of wall-clock capacity lost to input stalls corresponds to $\\$9,600$ per day, or $\\$288,000$ over 30 days. This is a capacity-cost estimate, not guaranteed cash savings: a storage investment pays back only if its cost is below the value of stalls actually removed and the recovered capacity reduces runtime or serves useful work."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team trains a vision model on a \\$50 TB dataset stored in cloud object storage (\\$0.02/GB-month) for 20 epochs across 4 training runs per year. Reading from object storage across a network boundary incurs a \\$0.09/GB data egress fee. Staging the dataset onto local NVMe caches costs \\$0.10/GB-month. What does the economic analysis reveal about streaming vs. staging?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Staging data once to local NVMe per run saves roughly \\$282,000 per year compared to streaming from object storage every epoch, breaking even after just 5 epochs per run. Streaming \\$50 TB across 20 epochs costs \\$90,000 in egress per run (\\$360,000/year for 4 runs) plus \\$12,000/year in object storage. Staging once per run incurs only \\$18,000/year in egress plus \\$60,000/year for local NVMe and \\$12,000/year in object storage (total \\$90,000/year), yielding \\$282,000 in net annual savings. The break-even point occurs at roughly 4.3 epochs per run. The streaming-is-cheaper option ignores the massive compounding cost of repeated multi-epoch egress. The egress-is-negligible claim inverts reality: egress charges dominate monthly capacity pricing. The tape archive suggestion is unviable for active multi-run training feeds.", "type": "single", "options": ["Streaming directly from object storage every epoch is cheaper because local NVMe drives add unnecessary provisioned storage overhead.", "Egress fees are negligible compared to monthly storage costs, making tier selection purely a matter of operational convenience.", "Staging data once to local NVMe per run saves roughly $\\$282,000$ per year compared to streaming from object storage every epoch, breaking even after just 5 epochs per run.", "The team should migrate the dataset to cold tape archive to eliminate both egress fees and provisioned storage costs."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the break-even point and cost savings of local NVMe data staging versus repeated cloud object storage streaming"]}, {"q": "An enterprise evaluates building an on-premises 1 PB parallel file system ($\\$3\\text{M to } \\$5\\text{M}$ capex plus $\\$500\\text{k/year}$ opex) versus storing the 1 PB corpus in cloud object storage ($\\$240\\text{k/year}$ storage) and paying $\\$0.09/\\text{GB}$ egress fees for 10 full-dataset reads per year ($\\$900\\text{k/year}$ egress). What is the realistic financial payback horizon for building the on-premises system under these parameters?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Between 4.7 and 7.8 years, because net annual cloud delivery savings ($\\$640\\text{k/year}$) require multiple years to amortize the multi-million dollar initial hardware expenditure. Cloud delivery costs $\\$1.14\\text{M/year}$ ($\\$240\\text{k}$ storage + $\\$900\\text{k}$ egress). Subtracting on-premises annual operations ($\\$500\\text{k}$) leaves net annual savings of $\\$640\\text{k/year}$. Dividing the $\\$3\\text{M}$ to $\\$5\\text{M}$ capex by $\\$640\\text{k}$ gives a break-even range of roughly 4.7 to 7.8 years. The 6-month claim is overly optimistic and ignores fixed facility, power, and operational overheads. The 20-year claim fails to account for the $\\$900\\text{k}$ annual egress cost in the cloud model. The perpetually free claim is factually false.", "type": "single", "options": ["Between 4.7 and 7.8 years, because net annual cloud delivery savings ($\\$640\\text{k/year}$) require multiple years to amortize the multi-million dollar initial hardware expenditure.", "Less than 6 months, because cloud egress fees make any on-premises storage investment instantly profitable.", "Exactly 20 years, because on-premises operational overhead exceeds all cloud storage and egress fees combined.", "There is no payback horizon because cloud object storage is perpetually free for research and training workloads."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate build-versus-buy trade-offs for large-scale storage infrastructure using capital expenditure, operational costs, and data egress fees"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the break-even point and cost savings of local NVMe data staging versus repeated cloud object storage streaming.", "You can now justify storage infrastructure capital investments by quantifying the opportunity cost of storage-induced accelerator idle time.", "You can now evaluate build-versus-buy trade-offs for large-scale storage infrastructure using capital expenditure, operational costs, and data egress fees.", "You can now explain the concept of data egress fees charged when reading datasets across cloud network boundaries."]}], "id": "v2-04-08-storage-economics", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-09-storage-for-serving": {"title": "Storage for Serving", "summary": "Calculate cold-start load times for model weights from different storage tiers and explain why parallel striping shortens them.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["Storage for Serving", "Retrieval indexes", "Storage Technologies That Move Tiers", "Storage Technologies That Move Tiers"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Storage for Serving** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate cold-start load times for model weights from different storage tiers and explain why parallel striping shortens them\n- Identify the shared resource that bounds a parallel weight load from host memory\n- Size a fleet-wide model rollout against shared storage tiers and identify its interference with co-tenant training\n- Compare the storage performance bottlenecks of vector retrieval graph traversal against training data streaming\n- Analyze the recall-latency-IOPS trade-off in graph-based high-dimensional vector search"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § Storage for Serving", "md": "Where full-corpus training streams datasets for weeks and writes checkpoints in bursts, the weight-resident serving baseline of a later section reads the model weights once when a replica starts or recovers and then answers requests out of HBM, before KV-cache offload and retrieval indexes add serving-time I/O of their own."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Retrieval indexes\n- Storage Technologies That Move Tiers\n\nPay attention to: cold-start load time as the serving-side storage metric (350 GB of FP16 weights); parallel loading across striped drives and the host-memory bound on a warm DRAM load; model rollout as a fan-out read burst that mirrors a checkpoint storm; vector index storage footprint (\\$1B 1536-d vectors approx 6.1 TB FP32)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A warm replica keeps the \\$350\\text{ GB}$ of weights in host DRAM. Each GPU's own host link could move its \\$43.75\\text{ GB}$ shard in about 0.7 seconds, yet the full load takes about 1.8 seconds. Explain why.", "md": "All eight shard transfers read from the same host memory, whose \\$200\\text{ GB/s}$ bandwidth must carry the whole \\$350\\text{ GB}$, which takes about 1.8 seconds. The per-GPU links run in parallel, so they are not the bottleneck; the shared host-memory bandwidth is. A warm replica therefore trades DRAM capacity for a host-memory-bound load that is still several times faster than a striped NVMe read."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A serving node must load the \\$350 GB of FP16 weights of a 175B-parameter model into accelerator memory. Which source gives the shortest cold start under the chapter's stated rates?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Striping the weights across eight drives divides the load into \\$43.75\\text{ GB}$ shards that load concurrently, so the load takes \\$43.75 / 7 \\approx 6.2\\text{ s}$. One faster drive still moves all \\$350\\text{ GB}$ serially (\\$350 / 14 = 25\\text{ s}$), and the shared tiers are slower still for a single node.", "type": "single", "options": ["Eight Gen4 NVMe drives read in parallel, each supplying one \\$43.75\\text{ GB}$ shard at \\$7\\text{ GB/s}$, for about 6.2 seconds.", "A single Gen5 NVMe drive at \\$14 GB/s, for about 25 seconds.", "The node's \\$4 GB/s share of the parallel file system, for about 87.5 seconds.", "A single object-storage client at \\$1 GB/s, for about 5.8 minutes."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate cold-start load times for model weights from different storage tiers and explain why parallel striping shortens them"]}, {"q": "A serving fleet of 100 replicas of a 175B-parameter model deploys a new version, so all replicas must read \\$35 TB of FP16 weights at once. Which statement correctly evaluates the shared sources?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The rollout reads \\$35 TB in one burst, so time is volume divided by the aggregate rate the source can give the fleet: \\$35 TB / 100 GB/s approx 5.8 minutes, or \\$35 TB / 1 TB/s = 35 seconds. The faster option starves every training job sharing the file system, the read-side mirror of a checkpoint storm, which is why fleets stage new versions locally, roll updates across replica groups, or let loaded replicas supply the rest.", "type": "single", "options": ["A \\$100\\text{ GB/s}$ object store finishes in about 5.8 minutes, while the \\$1\\text{ TB/s}$ parallel file system would take about 35 seconds but only by claiming the whole file system from co-tenant training jobs.", "The parallel file system is always the right source because 35 seconds is shorter, and serving reads cannot interfere with training jobs.", "The object store finishes in about 35 seconds because each replica reads only its own share of the weights.", "Rollout time is independent of the source tier because each replica's load is limited by its own PCIe link."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: size a fleet-wide model rollout against shared storage tiers and identify its interference with co-tenant training"]}, {"q": "How does the storage performance bottleneck for a graph-based vector retrieval index at billion-vector scale contrast with the primary bottleneck of an ML training data loader?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Vector retrieval traverses high-dimensional graph structures requiring high random-access IOPS and low point-lookup latency, whereas training data loaders require sustained high-bandwidth sequential streaming. Retrieval-augmented generation inverts the training access pattern: instead of streaming large contiguous shards sequentially, vector search algorithms (like HNSW or DiskANN) navigate sparse high-dimensional graph adjacency lists, issuing thousands of small, non-sequential pointer lookups where latency and random IOPS govern query response time. The sequential-streaming option inverts the two workloads. The burst-write claim confuses retrieval query serving with training checkpointing. The zero-storage claim ignores that billion-scale vector indexes span terabytes of storage.", "type": "single", "options": ["Vector retrieval is bottlenecked by sequential streaming bandwidth (GB/s), whereas training data loaders are bottlenecked by random \\$4 KB read IOPS.", "Vector retrieval requires continuous multi-terabyte burst writes, whereas training data loaders perform purely read-only operations.", "Vector retrieval is strictly compute-bound on matrix multiplication units and generates zero storage device interaction.", "Vector retrieval traverses high-dimensional graph structures requiring high random-access IOPS and low point-lookup latency, whereas training data loaders require sustained high-bandwidth sequential streaming."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare the storage performance bottlenecks of vector retrieval graph traversal against training data streaming"]}, {"q": "In graph-based approximate nearest-neighbor search such as HNSW or DiskANN, increasing target recall from \\$90\\%$ to \\$99\\%$ generally requires exploring more graph nodes, increasing random storage accesses and query latency.", "explanation": "True for graph-based ANN under otherwise comparable settings. Raising recall generally expands the search beam or visits more candidate nodes, increasing vector-distance computations and random DRAM or SSD accesses and therefore latency. This mechanism is specific to graph traversal; flat scans, IVF indexes, and PQ-based designs expose different controls and bottlenecks.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the recall-latency-IOPS trade-off in graph-based high-dimensional vector search"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A billion-entry vector index of 1536-dimensional FP32 embeddings consumes roughly \\$6.1 TB of raw vector data plus graph adjacency overhead. Explain why systems migrate from pure in-memory indexes (HNSW) to disk-backed graph indexes (DiskANN), and identify the primary hardware trade-off involved.", "md": "The raw vectors alone occupy about \\$6.1 TB. At 50 to 100 adjacency bytes per entry, minimal graph links add only \\$50 to \\$100 GB; identifiers, allocator overhead, slack, and replication can add more. A disk-backed graph index keeps compact navigation representations in DRAM and fetches other data from SSD, trading lower DRAM capacity for random SSD access latency. The actual memory reduction and latency penalty depend on the representation, cache, and search settings."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate cold-start load times for model weights from different storage tiers and explain why parallel striping shortens them.", "You can now identify the shared resource that bounds a parallel weight load from host memory.", "You can now size a fleet-wide model rollout against shared storage tiers and identify its interference with co-tenant training.", "You can now compare the storage performance bottlenecks of vector retrieval graph traversal against training data streaming."]}], "id": "v2-04-09-storage-for-serving", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-10-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Evaluate RAID parity and striping trade-offs for disposable ML caches under explicit durability and availability requirements.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-04", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Data Storage* (Vol II). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Evaluate RAID parity and striping trade-offs for disposable ML caches under explicit durability and availability requirements\n- Analyze training throughput regressions following accelerator compute upgrades using the I/O wall framework\n- Analyze the CPU-versus-I/O trade-offs of data compression and identify when decompression overhead degrades pipeline throughput\n- Evaluate why prefetch queues sized to average latency fail to prevent accelerator stalls under tail-latency variance\n- Compare naive full-copy dataset versioning against delta-encoded content-addressable snapshotting in terms of storage efficiency"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Data Storage, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Storage design errors are among the most expensive mistakes in ML infrastructure because they are discovered late, when training begins, and remediation requires either expensive hardware upgrades or time-consuming data reformatting. The following fallacies and pitfalls capture the most common errors that experienced storage engineers make when first encountering ML workloads."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: RAID parity versus striping under the cache tier's failure contract; accelerator compute upgrades exposing hidden storage bottlenecks; compression trade-offs on CPU-bound vs I/O-bound pipelines; prefetching limitations against P99 tail-latency spikes."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A training team upgrades its cluster from A100 to H100 GPUs, expecting a \\$3× end-to-end training speedup. However, wall-clock epoch time barely improves, and GPU utilization drops from \\$85\\% to \\$35\\%. Traces show shorter uninterrupted compute time, unchanged measured input-delivery rate, and increased time waiting for ready input batches, while communication and checkpoint pauses remain unchanged. Diagnose the measured bottleneck and explain why faster compute exposed it.", "md": "The traces establish an input-delivery limit: compute finishes sooner, but the unchanged pipeline does not supply ready batches at the new consumption rate. More of the iteration is therefore exposed input wait. Prefetching can hide delays within its queue window but cannot overcome a sustained delivery-rate deficit. The utilization and epoch-time figures alone would not identify this cause; the supplied timing measurements distinguish it from communication or checkpoint pauses. Profile individual read, decode, and transfer stages before selecting a remedy."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team selects RAID 5 for a disposable local training-data cache whose immutable source is durably stored elsewhere. The fleet can tolerate restaging after drive failure. Why might RAID 0 be a better throughput-and-capacity trade-off under this failure contract?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. A disposable cache need not own durability when its source remains available. RAID 0 removes parity overhead but increases exposure to cache loss and restaging. RAID 5 can still be justified by availability requirements; checkpoints awaiting durable replication are not disposable. Filesystem support and DMA are not disabled by RAID 5, and the stated endurance multiplier is unsupported.", "type": "single", "options": ["RAID 5 cannot be formatted with modern Linux filesystems such as ext4 or XFS.", "RAID 5 disables hardware DMA transfers, preventing the GPU from accessing host DRAM.", "The cache can be reconstructed from its durable source, so RAID 0 can aggregate drives without parity overhead when restaging disruption is acceptable.", "RAID 5 increases drive endurance wear by \\$10× compared to single-drive configurations."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate RAID parity and striping trade-offs for disposable ML caches under explicit durability and availability requirements"]}, {"q": "A team replaces a lighter codec with a denser codec on local-NVMe training shards. On this corpus, illustrative measurements give the lighter codec a 3:1 ratio and 1.5 GB/s decoded output per core, versus 4:1 and 0.5 GB/s for the denser codec. Before the change, worker profiles show decoding and augmentation saturating the host CPU and the ready-batch queue repeatedly draining. Afterward, decompression consumes more CPU time, the queue drains more often, and training step time increases although stored bytes decrease. What caused this measured regression?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The supplied worker profiles and ready-batch queues establish a host-processing limit before the change; additional decompression work further reduces completed-batch delivery. The denser codec saves stored bytes but requires more CPU work for the same decoded output under these corpus-specific measurements. These illustrative rates are not fixed properties of named compression levels. Compare output-equivalent stage rates and input wait when choosing a representation.", "type": "single", "options": ["Compressed shards cannot be read by NVMe controllers because flash drives require raw uncompressed binary blocks.", "The pipeline was already CPU-bound on data decoding and augmentation, so adding heavy decompression work saturated the host CPU, starving the GPU of assembled batches.", "The denser codec introduces non-deterministic bit flips that cause stochastic gradient descent to diverge.", "The parallel file system rejects compressed shards larger than 1 GB due to POSIX file locking constraints."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the CPU-versus-I/O trade-offs of data compression and identify when decompression overhead degrades pipeline throughput"]}, {"q": "Sizing a data loader's prefetch buffer depth based on average storage read latency ensures that training runs will not experience data stalls during multi-week training jobs on shared object storage.", "explanation": "False. Average latency does not bound tail stalls. A starting buffer budget is $Q_{\\text{prefetch}} = \\lceil T_{\\text{I/O,p99}} / T_{\\text{compute}} \\rceil$ plus margin, but longer or correlated delays can still drain it; sustained delivery bandwidth must also keep pace with consumption.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why prefetch queues sized to average latency fail to prevent accelerator stalls under tail-latency variance"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A team creates full \\$10 TB copies of a pretraining dataset for every snapshot version, creating 20 versions over six months (\\$200 TB total storage). Explain how content-addressable storage or delta encoding optimizes this workflow when successive versions differ by only \\$5\\%.", "md": "The first version requires \\$10 TB. Nineteen subsequent versions with \\$5\\% new content each add \\$0.5 TB, for \\$19.5 TB total before metadata and replication, saving \\$180.5 TB against 20 full copies. Content-addressable storage references unchanged blocks rather than copying them; the reduction assumes changes remain localized at the stored block or shard granularity."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now evaluate RAID parity and striping trade-offs for disposable ML caches under explicit durability and availability requirements.", "You can now analyze training throughput regressions following accelerator compute upgrades using the I/O wall framework.", "You can now analyze the CPU-versus-I/O trade-offs of data compression and identify when decompression overhead degrades pipeline throughput.", "You can now evaluate why prefetch queues sized to average latency fail to prevent accelerator stalls under tail-latency variance."]}], "id": "v2-04-10-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v2-04", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-04-99-test": {"title": "Test: Data Storage", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-04-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "An accelerator upgrade reduces compute time per batch while the input representation and storage delivery time remain fixed. What is the primary systems consequence for training cluster design?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Faster computation leaves less time to fetch the next batch. Overlap and prefetching hide latency within their buffering window, but cannot fix a sustained service-rate deficit. Automatic compression is not guaranteed, removing staging does not improve a slow source, and checkpoint frequency does not set input delivery throughput.", "type": "single", "options": ["Training pipelines automatically increase data compression ratios to prevent accelerator starvation without requiring architectural adjustments.", "Storage hierarchies can eliminate intermediate DRAM and NVMe caching tiers because modern network fabrics directly absorb the difference.", "Checkpoint intervals must be decreased proportionally to the compute growth to keep local NVMe buffers from overflowing.", "The compute window available to deliver each batch shrinks, so input I/O can become the bottleneck unless the measured delivery path keeps pace."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the systems consequences of compute throughput scaling significantly faster than persistent storage bandwidth"]}, {"q": "Order the sequence of operations that occurs in the traditional (non-GDS) storage I/O path when an accelerator requests a batch from local NVMe:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (1) The data loader process issues a POSIX read() system call traversing the VFS and filesystem drivers, (2) The NVMe controller initiates a DMA transfer of raw blocks into an OS kernel buffer in host DRAM, (3) The host CPU copies payload bytes from kernel space to user-space pinned memory, (4) The CPU programs a PCIe DMA transfer from host DRAM to GPU HBM, (5) The accelerator launches compute kernels on the newly arrived tensor resident in HBM. The traditional path begins with a file-system read call (1), stages data into an OS kernel buffer via storage DMA (2), requires a CPU-mediated memory copy into user space (3), executes a second DMA transfer over PCIe to GPU memory (4), and finally enables GPU computation (5).", "type": "order", "items": ["The NVMe controller initiates a DMA transfer of raw blocks into an OS kernel buffer in host DRAM", "The host CPU copies payload bytes from kernel space to user-space pinned memory", "The CPU programs a PCIe DMA transfer from host DRAM to GPU HBM", "The accelerator launches compute kernels on the newly arrived tensor resident in HBM."], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the multi-hop data movement and CPU mediation steps in the traditional storage I/O path"]}, {"q": "An in-memory LRU cache sized to \\$300 GB placed in front of a \\$3 TB uniformly shuffled pretraining dataset will achieve roughly a \\$90\\% hit rate after the first epoch warms the cache because all samples are reused across epochs.", "explanation": "False. A cache holding one-tenth of a uniformly shuffled corpus does not gain a 90 percent hit rate merely because samples recur in later epochs. Most cached samples are evicted before reuse. A fixed cyclic scan larger than the cache can approach zero hits; independently reshuffled epochs allow low but nonzero cross-epoch hits that depend on cache fraction and sample order.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why temporal-locality caching strategies such as LRU fail on uniformly streamed ML pretraining datasets"]}, {"q": "A team trains a vision model on a \\$50 TB dataset stored in cloud object storage (\\$0.02/GB-month) for 20 epochs across 4 training runs per year. Reading from object storage across a network boundary incurs a \\$0.09/GB data egress fee. Staging the dataset onto local NVMe caches costs \\$0.10/GB-month. What does the economic analysis reveal about streaming vs. staging?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Staging data once to local NVMe per run saves roughly \\$282,000 per year compared to streaming from object storage every epoch, breaking even after just 5 epochs per run. Streaming \\$50 TB across 20 epochs costs \\$90,000 in egress per run (\\$360,000/year for 4 runs) plus \\$12,000/year in object storage. Staging once per run incurs only \\$18,000/year in egress plus \\$60,000/year for local NVMe and \\$12,000/year in object storage (total \\$90,000/year), yielding \\$282,000 in net annual savings. The break-even point occurs at roughly 4.3 epochs per run. The streaming-is-cheaper option ignores the massive compounding cost of repeated multi-epoch egress. The egress-is-negligible claim inverts reality: egress charges dominate monthly capacity pricing. The tape archive suggestion is unviable for active multi-run training feeds.", "type": "single", "options": ["Streaming directly from object storage every epoch is cheaper because local NVMe drives add unnecessary provisioned storage overhead.", "Egress fees are negligible compared to monthly storage costs, making tier selection purely a matter of operational convenience.", "Staging data once to local NVMe per run saves roughly $\\$282,000$ per year compared to streaming from object storage every epoch, breaking even after just 5 epochs per run.", "The team should migrate the dataset to cold tape archive to eliminate both egress fees and provisioned storage costs."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the break-even point and cost savings of local NVMe data staging versus repeated cloud object storage streaming"]}, {"q": "A multi-modal training job combines \\$3\\text{ TB}$ of text, \\$50\\text{ TB}$ of images, and \\$200\\text{ TB}$ of video. The infrastructure team provisions storage bandwidth based exclusively on the video modality because video represents over \\$75\\%$ of total dataset capacity. What can be concluded about this sizing decision from the supplied information?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Retained corpus capacity does not determine per-step delivery demand. Sampling frequency, batch representation, and compute time set how many bytes each modality contributes to the feed, while transformations determine its decoding and augmentation work. Sum those demands across active streams and compare them with measured stage rates and buffer capacity. Video-only sizing omits that accounting, but the supplied corpus sizes do not prove a particular storage, host-DRAM, or decoding bottleneck.", "type": "single", "options": ["Adequacy cannot be established from corpus capacity alone; sizing must include the combined sampled-batch byte rate and decoding, augmentation, and buffering demand of all active modalities.", "The video stream will starve the parallel file system because image and text files use incompatible POSIX stripe sizes.", "The model will fail to converge because object storage cannot maintain ACID consistency when serving multiple modalities simultaneously.", "The training job will exhaust GPU HBM because video tensors automatically overwrite text embeddings during collation."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish retained corpus capacity from combined sampled-batch and transformation demand when sizing multimodal delivery"]}, {"q": "Order the sequence of steps executed during a tiered asynchronous checkpointing operation:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) The training orchestrator initiates a synchronized step barrier across all worker ranks, (3) Each rank snapshots its model weights and optimizer state shard into a local pinned DRAM buffer, (4) The training barrier releases and accelerators immediately resume forward-backward computation for the next step, (5) Each node flushes its pinned memory snapshot to local NVMe at full drive bandwidth, (1) Background daemons asynchronously transfer the local checkpoint shards from NVMe to the shared parallel file system. The process begins with a coordinated barrier (2), creates a fast in-memory snapshot (3), releases the compute barrier to resume training (4), writes the snapshot to local NVMe in the background (5), and asynchronously replicates the shard to shared storage in the background (1).", "type": "order", "items": ["The training orchestrator initiates a synchronized step barrier across all worker ranks", "Each rank snapshots its model weights and optimizer state shard into a local pinned DRAM buffer", "The training barrier releases and accelerators immediately resume forward-backward computation for the next step", "Each node flushes its pinned memory snapshot to local NVMe at full drive bandwidth.", "Background daemons asynchronously transfer the local checkpoint shards from NVMe to the shared parallel file system"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the multi-stage execution workflow of tiered asynchronous checkpointing"]}, {"q": "In large-scale distributed training, when hundreds of nodes simultaneously pause computation to write gigabytes of model weights and optimizer states to persistent storage, the resulting synchronized burst of write traffic is called a ____.", "explanation": "The correct term is checkpoint storm. A checkpoint storm occurs when all data-parallel or model-parallel workers simultaneously flush their memory shards to local or remote storage, creating extreme write contention that must be absorbed via tiered staging (such as writing to local NVMe before asynchronously copying to a parallel file system).", "type": "fill", "answer": ["The correct term is checkpoint storm"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the technical term for synchronized cluster-wide write bursts generated during distributed training state saves"]}, {"q": "Which statement precisely describes the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage (GDS)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. GDS establishes a direct DMA transfer path between NVMe storage (local or NVMe-oF) and GPU memory, eliminating the CPU-mediated bounce buffer in host DRAM while retaining CPU control-plane coordination. In the traditional path, data is copied from NVMe to a kernel buffer, copied by the CPU to user space, and then transferred to the GPU via PCIe DMA; GDS programs a direct peer-to-peer DMA between the NVMe controller and GPU HBM, skipping host DRAM payload copies while the CPU continues to issue and manage I/O control requests. The HBM caching option is physically impossible given dataset-to-HBM capacity ratios. The flash compression claim mischaracterizes GDS as in-storage compute. The bounce-buffer routing option describes the exact traditional bottleneck GDS was designed to eliminate.", "type": "single", "options": ["GDS caches the entire training corpus in GPU HBM to eliminate all storage I/O operations during training epochs.", "GDS compresses training tensors directly inside the NVMe flash controller using hardware acceleration.", "GDS routes network packets through host DRAM bounce buffers to perform real-time data integrity checksumming.", "GDS establishes a direct DMA transfer path between NVMe storage (local or NVMe-oF) and GPU memory, eliminating the CPU-mediated bounce buffer in host DRAM while retaining CPU control-plane coordination."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural data-path modification introduced by GPUDirect Storage compared to traditional CPU-mediated I/O"]}, {"q": "An enterprise evaluates building an on-premises 1 PB parallel file system ($\\$3\\text{M to } \\$5\\text{M}$ capex plus $\\$500\\text{k/year}$ opex) versus storing the 1 PB corpus in cloud object storage ($\\$240\\text{k/year}$ storage) and paying $\\$0.09/\\text{GB}$ egress fees for 10 full-dataset reads per year ($\\$900\\text{k/year}$ egress). What is the realistic financial payback horizon for building the on-premises system under these parameters?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Between 4.7 and 7.8 years, because net annual cloud delivery savings ($\\$640\\text{k/year}$) require multiple years to amortize the multi-million dollar initial hardware expenditure. Cloud delivery costs $\\$1.14\\text{M/year}$ ($\\$240\\text{k}$ storage + $\\$900\\text{k}$ egress). Subtracting on-premises annual operations ($\\$500\\text{k}$) leaves net annual savings of $\\$640\\text{k/year}$. Dividing the $\\$3\\text{M}$ to $\\$5\\text{M}$ capex by $\\$640\\text{k}$ gives a break-even range of roughly 4.7 to 7.8 years. The 6-month claim is overly optimistic and ignores fixed facility, power, and operational overheads. The 20-year claim fails to account for the $\\$900\\text{k}$ annual egress cost in the cloud model. The perpetually free claim is factually false.", "type": "single", "options": ["Between 4.7 and 7.8 years, because net annual cloud delivery savings ($\\$640\\text{k/year}$) require multiple years to amortize the multi-million dollar initial hardware expenditure.", "Less than 6 months, because cloud egress fees make any on-premises storage investment instantly profitable.", "Exactly 20 years, because on-premises operational overhead exceeds all cloud storage and egress fees combined.", "There is no payback horizon because cloud object storage is perpetually free for research and training workloads."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate build-versus-buy trade-offs for large-scale storage infrastructure using capital expenditure, operational costs, and data egress fees"]}, {"q": "A team generates a \\$1\\text{ TB}$ synthetic text dataset. Retained provenance adds \\$40\\%$ metadata overhead, and 3 verifier models each read the payload once without materializing another full copy. What persistent capacity and total pipeline traffic are required?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Persistent capacity is \\$1.4\\text{ TB}$: the \\$1\\text{ TB}$ payload plus \\$0.4\\text{ TB}$ of provenance. Pipeline traffic is \\$4.4\\text{ TB}$: those \\$1.4\\text{ TB}$ of writes plus three \\$1\\text{ TB}$ verifier reads. Verification passes do not multiply capacity unless their outputs are materialized as additional full datasets.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$1.4 TB of persistent capacity and \\$4.4 TB of traffic: \\$1 TB payload write, \\$0.4 TB metadata write, and \\$3 TB of verifier reads.", "Exactly \\$1.0 TB, because verifier models run entirely in GPU registers without generating persistent storage artifacts.", "Roughly \\$0.4 TB, because synthetic data compresses more efficiently than natural language text.", "Roughly \\$14\\text{ TB}$, because every verifier model stores a full \\$350\\text{ GB}$ copy of its internal neural activations for every sample."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish persistent storage amplification from read and write traffic in a verified synthetic-data pipeline"]}, {"q": "If a cluster monitoring dashboard indicates that average storage read bandwidth matches the aggregate throughput predicted by the pipeline equation, storage performance issues can be conclusively ruled out as the cause of low GPU utilization.", "explanation": "False. Average bandwidth metrics mask transient tail-latency spikes, worker-side lock contention, metadata serialization, and prefetch buffer underruns. Even if aggregate gigabytes-per-second over a 10-minute window matches the theoretical target, microsecond-level and millisecond-level I/O jitter can cause individual GPUs to stall repeatedly at step boundaries, driving down utilization. Diagnostics must evaluate per-step wait-for-data distributions (P95/P99 stall times) rather than aggregate bandwidth averages alone.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why average bandwidth metrics fail to rule out storage bottlenecks without fine-grained tail-latency and stall-ratio profiling"]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v2-04-99-test", "chapter": "v2-04", "minutes": 10}});
